  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Useful as a clear signed byte envelope, but weak as a direct answer to route-leak pressure: policy scope, onward-restraint, and leak evidence are all delegated to unspecified payload or wrapper protocols, while unknown-hard-reject hurts long-term interoperability.",
    "strengths": [
      "Deterministic CBOR plus a mandatory signature gives stable tamper-evident bytes.",
      "The envelope/payload boundary is explicit and avoids hidden global state or a central registry.",
      "Recursive wrapping could carry relay-local evidence if higher-layer routing protocols define it."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "The specimen does not itself define route-policy scope, onward-restraint promises, or leak-violation evidence.",
      "Signature bytes are opaque at the envelope layer, so audit depends on external algorithm and identity conventions.",
      "Unknown-pCID hard rejection can prevent preservation of newer policy or wrapper objects."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Signed reachability data could be mistaken for signed authorization unless payload semantics are very explicit.",
      "Older peers may drop future routing-policy evidence instead of retaining it for later audit.",
      "Long-term verification is fragile if signature typing and key migration remain outside the envelope."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "What concrete payload or wrapper pCID would encode transit scope and onward-restraint promises?",
      "What local record is kept when an unknown-pCID envelope is rejected, and is it enough for later leak audit?",
      "How are signature algorithm discovery and key rotation preserved over multi-decade evolution?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
